@page "/docs/common/forms"

@* Forms and validation: one form for static and interactive pages, what fields read from the
   model, checks before and after a form post, columns, read-only and disabled forms, the shared
   caveats on disabled fields and values a form post can change, and errors. From
   docs/components.md ("Forms") and getting-started.md; the caveats checked against Checkbox,
   CheckboxGroup and ColorPicker (hidden fields that post a read-only value). *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="forms">
    <Lead>
        <Code>Form</Code> wraps Blazor's <Code>EditForm</Code>, so one form works in every render mode. On static pages it
        sends a form post, even with JavaScript off; on interactive pages it validates as the user types. Each field reads
        its label and rules from your model.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">A form that posts</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            On <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">static pages</Anchor>, a form post fills your model by name. Set
            <Code>FormName</Code> on the form, and put <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm]</Code> with the same
            <Code>FormName</Code> on the model property:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" HighlightLines="1,9-10">@Basic</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>OnValidSubmit</Code> runs on the server once the model is valid, in every render mode. On interactive
            pages the form checks each field as it changes, and submits without a full reload.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            On static pages the errors come from the last form post. A field's errors go once its value changes, and the
            next form post checks it again. In a Combobox or MultiSelect, the errors go when the user chooses an option,
            not while they type a search. A <Code>FormErrors</Code> summary keeps listing every error until the next form
            post. With JavaScript off, the field errors stay too.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Labels and rules from the model</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Each field reads its property's attributes:</TextBlock>
        <Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Sets</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>[Display(Name)]</code></td><td>The label. Without it, the label is the property name split into words.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>[Required]</code></td><td>The required mark, and the check</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>[EmailAddress]</code>, <code>[Phone]</code>, <code>[Url]</code></td><td>The input type</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>[StringLength]</code>, <code>[Range]</code></td><td>The limits</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </Prose>
        <Code Block Language="csharp">@Model</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Without <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>, the label splits the property name into words ("Zip code"), but validation
            messages use the name as written ("The ZipCode field is required."). To make the messages match the label, add
            <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Checked before and after a form post</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The browser checks required fields, lengths, ranges, emails and URLs before the form posts. The server then
            checks every rule and shows its messages under each field. Rules the browser can't check, such as a custom
            attribute, run only on the server.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            With <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">IzzyBlazor's script</Anchor>, the browser shows the message from
            your attribute's <Code>ErrorMessage</Code>, such as your <Code>[Required]</Code> message, so it matches the server's.
            With JavaScript off, the browser shows its default message.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            A field's <Code>Required</Code> parameter shows the mark and asks the browser to check the field. The server
            reads only <Code>[Required]</Code> on the model, so put every rule the server must enforce there.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Columns</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A form can lay its fields out in up to four columns. The columns follow the form's own width, not the screen's,
            so one form fits a page, a dialog and a drawer. Set <Code>Columns</Code> on the form to turn them on.
        </TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="form-columns" />
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Two columns start at 32rem of form width, three at 48rem and four at 64rem. Below 32rem, the form has one column.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Each field fills one column. To give a field the whole row, set <Code>ColSpan="GridSpan.Full"</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>To start a field on a new row, set <Code>NewRow</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Anything that isn't a field, such as text, <Code>FormErrors</Code> or <Code>FormActions</Code>, fills the whole row.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Groups, actions and errors</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/fieldset">Fieldset</Anchor> groups related fields under a <Code>Label</Code>. It can set its own <Code>Columns</Code>, or <Code>Dividers</Code> between rows.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>FormActions</Code> is the row of buttons at the end of the form. When it comes last in a form that fills a dialog or drawer, it becomes the footer and stays in view.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>FormErrors</Code> lists every message under "Please fix the following". It takes focus after a failed submit, with JavaScript off too.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Code>FieldError For="() =&gt; Model.Email" Id="email-error"</Code> shows one property's messages, for an input of your own. Point the input's <Code>aria-describedby</Code> at its <Code>Id</Code>.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Read-only and disabled</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A read-only form shows its values but doesn't let the user change them, as on a page that shows a record
            before editing it. The values still post with the form. To make every field read-only, set
            <Code>ReadOnly</Code> on the form.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            HTML has no read-only select, checkbox, radio or range. So in a read-only form, a select shows its value in a
            read-only text field. Checkboxes, switches, radios, segments and sliders are disabled, and a hidden field posts
            each one's value.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            In a disabled form, the user can't use any field, and the fields post nothing. To disable every field, set
            <Code>Disabled</Code> on the form.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>Neither setting changes the buttons, so show the ones that fit.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A field's own <Code>Disabled</Code> works the same way as the form's.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Text fields and selects also have their own <Code>ReadOnly</Code>. Checkboxes, switches, radios, segments and sliders don't.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Disabled fields and form posts</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A disabled field posts nothing. After a form post on static pages, its property has its default value, such as
            <Code>null</Code>, <Code>0</Code> or <Code>false</Code>, not the value the field showed. Keep that value on the
            server rather than saving it from the form post.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Values a form post can change</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A form post comes from the browser, so the user can change anything in it. That includes hidden fields, read-only
            values and values for disabled fields. Validate them on the server, and don't trust a value only because the
            user couldn't edit it on the page.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Required marks and label position</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A required mark tells the user which fields they must fill in. By default, required fields show an asterisk.
            When most fields are required, mark the others "(optional)" instead, or show no marks. Set
            <Code>RequiredMark</Code> on the form to choose.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>To put labels beside the fields, at the start, set <Code>Layout="FormLayout.Horizontal"</Code>. Labels move beside the fields from 640px up. With <Code>Columns</Code>, they stay on top.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>To hide a field's label but keep it for screen readers, as in a one-field search form, set <Code>HideLabel</Code> on the field.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>: every parameter, and <Code>Validator</Code> to replace DataAnnotations.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/text-field">TextField</Anchor>: types, add-ons, passwords and validation.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>: the words forms use, such as "(optional)".</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</Anchor>: a form post that fails or arrives empty, an empty selection, a redirect after saving.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Basic = """
        <Form Model="Signup" FormName="signup" OnValidSubmit="SaveAsync">
            <FormErrors />
            <TextField @bind-Value="Signup.Email" />
            <Select @bind-Value="Signup.Plan" Placeholder="Choose a plan" />
            <Button Submit>Create account</Button>
        </Form>

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "signup")]
            public SignupModel? Signup { get; set; }

            protected override void OnInitialized() => Signup ??= new();
        }
        """;

    private const string Model = """
        public sealed class SignupModel
        {
            [Required(ErrorMessage = "Enter your email"), EmailAddress]
            public string? Email { get; set; }      // "Email", type="email", required

            [StringLength(10)]
            public string? ZipCode { get; set; }    // "Zip code", maxlength="10"

            [Required(ErrorMessage = "Choose a plan")]
            public Plan? Plan { get; set; }         // a select of the enum's values
        }
        """;
}
